// @run
// @log nul
// @log b
// @info `a` is never reassigned; use `const` instead of `let`
// @info `c` is never reassigned; use `const` instead of `let`
// CONTRACT (must-RUN, D884): a LITERAL-UNION alias is the seventh atom of the `??` leaf merge,
// and it joins a set of `null`s and nothing else.
//
// `anonLeafAnnAtomOf` reads the operand's declared field type off the SPELLING — a row's
// `{r: i32}` and `{r: boolean}` fields are both code 0, so the code cannot say it — and
// admitted six scalar names. `type K = "a" | "b"` is not one, so the first block below had a
// one-member family and stayed `emitProgram: bare null needs a struct-typed context`. The
// merged set `K|null` is the code-30 niche `nameFieldCode` already spells for the DECLARED
// twin (`type R = { r: K | null }` runs), and the mint records the non-null alias as the
// field's companion exactly as `pushFieldRow`'s declared path does.
//
// THE TWO BLOCKS USE **DIFFERENT FIELD NAMES**, and that separation is the point rather than
// tidiness. `anonLeafPolyUnionSetOpen` collects every SAME-FIELD-SET sibling's atom, so on one
// field name the second block's `{r: "b"}` hands the first block's family a `string` atom —
// a set master can already spell (`string|null`, code 20) — and the whole file then RUNS on
// master and pins nothing. Measured: the one-field-name draft of this file builds and prints
// the same two lines under master's own seed. `r` and `q` keep each block its own question.
//
// THE SECOND BLOCK IS THE PRICE THE UNGATED ATOM CHARGED, and it runs on master too — that is
// what makes it a pin. A VALUE's atom is read off the value, so `{q: "b"}` contributes
// `string` and code 20 carries `string|null`. Letting `K` in unconditionally made that set
// `K|string|null`, which `nameFieldCode` has no code for, so the mint declined, the field fell
// back to `anonFieldCode`'s answer, and a RUNNING program became the same loud reject the
// first block used to give. `K` and `string` are different reps (an interned i32 id against a
// string ref); a set holding both would need a box the niche codes cannot give.

type K = "a" | "b"

// ---- D884's own case: no second literal on field `r`, so the annotation is the other half ----
function rd() {
  let a: { r: K } | null = null
  const g1 = a ?? { r: null }
  if g1.r == null { print("nul") } else { print("has") }
}
rd() // nul

// ---- the PRICE: field `q` has a literal operand, whose `string` atom `K` must not poison ----
function pd() {
  let c: { q: K } | null = { q: "b" }
  const g2 = c ?? { q: null }
  if g2.q == null { print("nul") } else { print(g2.q) }
}
pd() // b
